body:
On an example below, should we see "PASS" on `#output`?:
https://jsfiddle.net/64eaqtr7/
```javascript
function onload_event(iframe) {
    return new Promise(resolve => {
        iframe.addEventListener('load', ()=>{resolve();}, {once: true});
    });
}

(async function runTest() {
    const iframe = document.createElement('iframe');
    document.body.appendChild(iframe);

    iframe.srcdoc = '<!doctype html>';
    await onload_event(iframe);
    const promise = iframe.contentWindow.Promise;

    iframe.srcdoc = '';
    await onload_event(iframe);

    promise
        .resolve()
        .then()
        .then(()=>{
            document.querySelector('#output').textContent = 'PASS';
        });
})();
```

`promise` is the Promise constructor on the inactive (non [fully-active](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsers.html#fully-active)) browsing context, and `resolve()` returns a resolved promise on it.
When `then()` is called, we run [PerformPromiseThen](https://tc39.github.io/ecma262/#sec-performpromisethen), and run [EnqueueJob](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/webappapis.html#enqueuejob(queuename,-job,-arguments)) for a PromiseReactionJob to perform the reaction, where \[\[Handler\]\] of the job is undefined. At the step 7.1 of EnqueueJob, we check if an appropriate browsing context is fully-active, and if it's not fully-active, we cancel the job.

Then, according to [a discussion of the appropriate browsing context](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/1426#issuecomment-340071080), we should use \[\[Handler\]\]'s context for the check, but it's undefined on the example. If we choose the promise's creation context (the result of [GetFunctionRealm](https://tc39.github.io/ecma262/#sec-getfunctionrealm) of the constructor) as the second most appropriate one, the PromiseReactionJob will be cancelled, and the resulting Promise of `then()` is not resolved nor rejected.
